# death_if_fails rescaled for real per-act risk (drafts/repeats/rescale_death.py)
# Chroma library, modern Earth: TITLE STAGES. Generic moments that any holder of a kind of title meets (holds: kind:<kind>),
# in three stages of tenure: new (tenure: 0-1), settled (2-7), senior or veteran (8-60). Emren's point 6 (2026-10-05 20:39):
# "beginning of scout role can be different than senior/veteran scout. Event can allow players explore their title and perk
# opportunities, or transition from the stage of title." Brief: drafts/next2/BRIEF.md. Syntax: header of build.py.
# {title} stands for the held title's say form from the catalogue ("a nurse", "on the team", "married", "a parent"). Because
# those forms mix nouns, phrases and adjectives, it is always written after "has been" or "have been", where every form reads.
# drops: {title} on an option means the act ends whichever title of that kind is held (a note for the engine).
# Calls: each of the ten pairs three times (every color called 12 times); cross cycles s1 x8, s2 x8, s3 x7, s4 x7.
# Ages: careers, faith, partner and children are adult moments (18 and over); community moments start at 12 and are worded
# for a teenager in a troop, a team or a band as much as for an adult on a committee.

# ================================================================ CAREER (holds: kind:career)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new: learning the ropes (calls UG, s1)

== nobody wrote down how it is really done
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 2
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-72
calls: UG
holds: kind:career
tenure: 0-1
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, mentor
earth: you have been {title} for a few weeks: the manual says one thing, and the people who have done it for years do another
times: Nearly everyone new in a job, once per job, in the first weeks or months; US baby boomers held about 12 jobs between 18 and 54 (BLS NLSY79)
scene: {N} has been {title} for two months and has learned that the training covered half the job. The other half lives in the heads of people who have done it for years.
scene W: There is a handbook, a code and a right way, and {N} has read all of it. Nobody on the floor seems to work from it.
scene U: {N} keeps a notebook in a back pocket: who knows what, which steps get skipped, what the manual never mentions. It fills up fast.
scene B: {N} has already worked out who really runs the place, and it is not the name on the office door.
scene R: {N} would rather get stuck in and learn by getting it wrong a few times than sit through another week of watching.
scene G: {colleague} has done this work for twenty years and moves through it without hurrying, the way old hands do. {N} watches, and copies.
- W | learn the handbook by heart and do every task exactly as it is written | habit | v: conformity, security | chance: 88
- U | keep a notebook of every trick the old hands use, and test each one | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 85
- B | make yourself useful to whoever really runs the place | v: power, security | grants: friend in power | chance: 75
- R | take on the hardest job on the list before being signed off for it | v: stimulation, achievement | body: light | closed: approval: working beyond one's training; backfire: it goes wrong and the newcomer is taken off the floor | chance: 65
- G | ask the oldest hand if you can work beside them for a season | door | v: tradition, benevolence | grants: mentor | mark: learned a skill | chance: 72
- W>U | ask your supervisor for a proper training plan, with dates and sign-offs | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 80
- U>B | work out which parts of the job the managers actually check | v: achievement, power | chance: 82
- B>R | skip the slow parts nobody checks and get to the parts you enjoy | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | self_control: - | closed: approval: cutting corners in training; backfire: a skipped step goes wrong in front of a supervisor | chance: 80
- R>G | go out with the crew after the shift, and mostly listen | habit | v: benevolence, hedonism | mark: made a friend | chance: 90
- G>W | follow the crew's habit of skipping the slow safety step, as the old hands do | v: tradition, conformity | closed: law: skipping a safety rule; backfire: an inspection, or an injury | mark: gave in to pressure | chance: 75
works: Within a month {N} can do the job without looking round for someone to ask.
works: {colleague} starts calling {N} by a nickname, which on this floor means something.
fails: {N} learns the wrong way first, and has to unlearn it in front of everyone.
fails: The weeks blur, and {N} still feels like a visitor in someone else's workplace.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new: the first mistake in front of others (calls WR, s2)

== the first mistake with everyone watching
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 2
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-72
calls: WR
holds: kind:career
tenure: 0-1
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, rival
earth: you have been {title} for a few weeks when you make your first real mistake, and the whole place sees it
times: Most people new in a job, once per job, in the first months; a first mistake in front of others is part of learning most jobs (estimate)
scene: {N} has been {title} for six weeks when {N} gets something wrong in front of everyone, and the silence afterwards is the loudest thing in the room.
scene W: {N} knows exactly which rule was broken, and that a newcomer is judged on what they do in the next hour.
scene U: Even with every eye on {N}, part of {Ns} mind is already working out where the step went wrong, and why.
scene B: {boss} saw it. So did {rival}, who wanted this job and did not get it.
scene R: {Ns} face burns. The urge is to laugh it off, or walk out, or shout at whoever left the thing where it was.
scene G: Everyone who has done this work for long has a first-mistake story. {N} has just been handed one.
- W | own it at once, out loud, and put it right before anything else | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: owned up | self_control: + | chance: 82
- U | write down exactly what happened and work out the cause before going to sleep | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 90
- B | let it be thought the mix-up came from the shift before | v: security, power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: blaming others; backfire: the shift before has the log to prove otherwise | chance: 60
- R | laugh at yourself before anyone else can, and carry straight on | v: hedonism, self-direction | act: laugh at themselves before anyone else can, and carry straight on | chance: 85
- G | let the sting pass, and come in tomorrow as on any other day | v: tradition (acceptance), security | self_control: + | chance: 75
- W>B | write the incident up so the record shows you followed procedure | v: security, power | closed: approval: shading an official record; backfire: the camera footage shows otherwise | chance: 60
- U>R | find the fastest fix, then slip out before it turns into a meeting | v: hedonism, self-direction | self_control: - | chance: 75
- B>G | bring cakes in next morning and let the mistake become a crew joke | v: benevolence, security | chance: 88
- R>W | go straight to the person it affected and apologise to their face | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 85
- G>U | ask the oldest hand what their own first mistake was | door | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 85
works: By the end of the week it is a story, not a stain, and {N} is the one telling it.
works: {boss} mentions it once, briefly, and seems to think better of {N} for how it was handled.
fails: The mistake follows {N} around for weeks, brought up whenever anything else goes wrong.
fails: A formal note goes on file, and {N} lies awake replaying the moment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- settled: a chance to specialise or take on more (calls WB, s3)

== the job could grow in two directions
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 1
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 20-75
calls: WB
holds: kind:career
tenure: 2-7
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, mentor
earth: you have been {title} for a few years, you are good at it, and the job could grow in more than one direction
times: Many workers, once per job, two to seven years in; the median time with one employer is about 4 years in the US (BLS)
scene: {N} has been {title} for four years and is good at it, and {boss} has asked what {N} wants next. There is more than one answer.
scene W: A supervisor's post is opening: a desk, a rota to keep, other people's mistakes to answer for. {N} would do it properly.
scene U: There is a corner of the work that nobody here truly understands, and {N} has been reading about it at night.
scene B: {N} has a list of the customers, suppliers and people upstairs who now ask for {N} by name. That list is worth something.
scene R: {N} could stay where the work is most fun, or blow it all up and go out alone.
scene G: {mentor} once said a trade is like a tree: it grows taller or it grows wider, but it has to grow. {N} has been wondering which.
- W | apply for the supervisor's post and do the training it asks for | identity | v: achievement, conformity | title: shift manager | requires: trusted with the keys | without: approval | lacking: .5 | chance: 30
- U | study for the specialist qualification at night, for as long as it takes | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 55
- B | quietly build up your own clients, the kind who would follow anywhere | v: power, security | grants: loyal clientele | chance: 70
- R | hand in your notice and set up a firm of your own | identity | v: self-direction, stimulation | title: founder of a firm | mark: took a wild risk | requires: savings | without: means | lacking: .4 | chance: 12
- G | stay where you are, and grow deeper at what you already do well | v: tradition, security | chance: 80
- W>R | ask in writing for a rota that leaves one weekday free each week | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 65
- U>G | take on a trainee and teach them what took you years to learn | binds | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
- B>W | make sure you are the one trusted to open up and lock up | v: power, security | chance: 80
- R>U | throw yourself at the hardest problem nobody else will touch | v: stimulation, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 70
- G>B | let the people you have looked after for years spread your name | v: power, benevolence | chance: 45
works: The new direction takes, and within a year the work has a shape that is plainly {Ns} own.
works: {boss} agrees, and puts it in writing before anyone can change their mind.
fails: The door {N} chose stays shut this round, and someone else walks through it.
fails: {N} takes on more than the week can hold, and something important slips.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- settled: a plateau and a rival (calls UR, s4)

== the colleague who started the same month
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: 1
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 20-75
calls: UR
holds: kind:career
tenure: 2-7
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: rival, boss, colleague, parent
earth: you have been {title} for some years, the work runs on its own, and the person who started the same month as you is pulling ahead
times: Many workers, once per job, a few years in, when a peer is promoted first; about 1 in 7 employees of US tech firms is promoted in a year (Pave)
scene: {N} has been {title} for five years. The work runs itself now, and {rival}, who started the same month, has just been given the project {N} wanted.
scene W: {N} has done everything asked, on time and properly, year after year. It is not clear that anyone has noticed.
scene U: {N} can predict every working day before it happens. A mind the work used to stretch now idles through it.
scene B: {rival} is not better at the job. {rival} is better at being seen doing it, and {N} has started taking notes.
scene R: On the drive in, {N} catches {Ns} own face in the mirror and does not like how bored it looks.
scene G: Every trade has its long flat stretch, {parent} used to say: the years when nothing shows above ground and the roots go down.
- W | report the rival for the small rule they bend every week | v: conformity, power | closed: approval: informing on a colleague; backfire: the team finds out who told | chance: 70
- U | teach yourself the skill the job will need in five years' time | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | chance: 78
- B | make sure the bosses know who really did the rival's best work | v: power, achievement | mark: made an enemy | chance: 65
- R | apply for something completely different, just to feel the jolt | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 60
- G | accept the flat years, and put your heart into the life outside work | v: tradition (acceptance), hedonism | chance: 75
- W>G | offer to train the newcomers, as the old hands once trained you | v: benevolence, tradition | grants: teaching | act: offer to train the newcomers, as the old hands once trained them | chance: 82
- U>W | write up the better way you have found and send it through proper channels | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 65
- B>U | take the rival to lunch and find out exactly how they got ahead | door | v: achievement | chance: 85
- R>B | tell the boss you want the next big project, or you will leave | v: power, self-direction | chance: 55
- G>R | take the long holiday you have been saving up, and see how you feel after | v: hedonism | chance: 85
works: Something shifts, a new piece of work or a new skill, and the days start to have edges again.
works: {boss} sees it, and the next big job comes to {N} without being asked for.
fails: The next project goes to {rival} as well, and the talk about it in the canteen is hard to sit through.
fails: The flat stretch goes on, and {N} starts counting the months to the next change.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- senior: the old guard and the new ways (calls BG, s1)

== the newcomers do it a new way
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: .5
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 26-80
calls: BG
holds: kind:career
tenure: 8-60
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, a trainee
earth: you have been {title} for many years, the newcomers do the work a new way, and they look to you to bless it or block it
times: About half of long-serving workers, once per job, after eight years or more; median tenure is nearly 10 years for US workers aged 55 to 64 (BLS)
share: .5
scene: {N} has been {title} for longer than the place has had its present shape. Now the newcomers do the work a new way, and they keep glancing at {N}.
scene W: The old way had reasons, written in near-misses and complaints nobody else remembers. {N} remembers.
scene U: The new method is faster on paper. {N} wants to see it on a bad day before deciding anything.
scene B: Whoever decides which way stays will matter here for years. Right now that could be {N}, if {N} wants it.
scene R: The newcomers are loud and quick and a little careless, and {N} catches {Ns} own younger self in them.
scene G: Old ways and new ones have always gone round like this, {N} thinks; each new crop believes it invented the harvest.
- W | report the newcomers' shortcuts to head office, by name | v: conformity, security | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: going over the team's heads; backfire: the crew closes ranks against the old hand | chance: 65
- U | run both methods side by side for a month and count the results | v: achievement, universalism | chance: 80
- B | back the new way early, so you are the one who brought it in | v: power, achievement | grants: inner circle at work | chance: 75
- R | tell the newcomers loudly that half their shortcuts will hurt someone | v: self-direction, security | closed: approval: shouting at junior staff; backfire: a complaint goes to personnel | chance: 80
- G | let the new way prove itself or fail, as such things always have | v: tradition (acceptance) | chance: 65
- W>U | write down everything the old way knows before it is lost | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 88
- U>B | get into the new system's settings and quietly keep the old way running | v: power, security | closed: approval: tampering with a work system; backfire: an audit traces the change | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 60
- B>R | take the redundancy money while it is generous, and go travelling | v: hedonism, self-direction | drops: {title} | chance: 85
- R>G | take the youngest newcomer under your wing, mistakes and all | binds | v: benevolence | chance: 85
- G>W | keep the old way for the jobs where it matters, and let go of the rest | v: security, tradition | chance: 72
works: The work settles into a shape that keeps the best of both, and the newcomers start asking {N} first.
works: Whatever {N} chose holds, and within a year nobody remembers there was a quarrel.
fails: The newcomers go round {N}, and decisions start being made in rooms {N} is not in.
fails: The change goes badly, and everyone remembers whose side {N} was on.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- senior: a last big chance, or handing over (calls BR, s2)

== the biggest job of a career, late in the day
tier: everyday
at: employer
rate: .5
once: yes
stages: young_adult+
age: 30-80
calls: BR
holds: kind:career
tenure: 8-60
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: years
roles: boss, partner, mentor, colleague
earth: you have been {title} for many years, and the biggest job of your career arrives just as you were starting to think about handing over
times: Some long-serving workers, perhaps 1 in 4, once, late in a career; big late projects tend to go to the most experienced (estimate)
share: .25
scene: {N} has been {title} for many years when the biggest piece of work of a whole career is offered, just as {N} had started to think about handing over.
scene W: {N} promised {partner} the hours would get shorter from now on. This would make them longer for two years at least.
scene U: {N} knows more about this work than almost anyone. The question is whether that is still enough, at this age, at this pace.
scene B: This is the one that would put {Ns} name on something for good. There will not be another offer like it.
scene R: {N} feels a jolt of the old hunger, the one that pulled {N} into this work in the first place.
scene G: {N} has trained half the people who could do it. Perhaps it is their turn, and {Ns} turn to watch from the side.
- W | take it on, with a named successor working alongside from the first day | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 70
- U | take it, and write the method down as you go so it outlives you | v: achievement, universalism | act: take it, and write the method down along the way so it outlives them | chance: 75
- B | ask for a stake in the firm as the price of doing it | binds | v: power, achievement | grants: stake in the firm | chance: 50
- R | say yes on the spot, and throw every hour you have at it | binds | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 70
- G | turn it down, and recommend the best person you ever trained | v: benevolence, tradition | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 85
- W>B | accept, on condition that the credit carries your name in writing | v: power, security | grants: name in the field | chance: 70
- U>R | work out a way to do it part time, from home, on your own hours | v: self-direction | chance: 60
- B>G | take it, and put every penny towards a place by the sea for later | v: security, hedonism | grants: place by the sea | chance: 65
- R>W | say plainly that you are done being {title}, and hand over your work | v: self-direction, benevolence | drops: {title} | takes: trusted with the keys | chance: 90
- G>U | ask your old mentor what they wish they had done at your age | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 75
works: The choice sits well, and a year on {N} can say why it was the right one.
works: The work, or the handing over, goes better than {N} feared, and people say so.
fails: {N} chooses, and spends months wondering about the other road.
fails: The pace is too much, and by winter {Ns} body is keeping the score.

# ================================================================ COMMUNITY (holds: kind:community; from 12, for teenagers and adults)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new: finding who to trust (calls WB, s3)

== the pecking order nobody mentions
tier: everyday
rate: 2
once: yes
stages: juvenile+
age: 12-85
calls: WB
holds: kind:community
tenure: 0-1
tone: mixed
life: community, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, rival, elder
earth: you have been {title} for a few weeks, and the group has rules nobody wrote down and a pecking order nobody mentions
times: Nearly everyone who joins a club, team or group, once per group, in the first weeks; about 1 in 4 US adults volunteers through an organisation (AmeriCorps)
scene: {N} has been {title} for a few weeks and has worked out that the group has a pecking order nobody mentions and rules nobody wrote down.
scene W: There is a right way to belong here, and {N} can feel it: where to stand, when to speak, who goes first. {N} wants to learn it properly.
scene U: {N} watches who talks to whom, who gets listened to and who gets quietly talked over, and draws a map of it in {Ns} head.
scene B: Two people really run this group, and they do not like each other. Whoever {N} sides with first will decide a great deal.
scene R: {N} does not care about the pecking order. {N} came for the thing itself and wants to get on with it.
scene G: The group is older than any of its members. {N} can feel the habits of people long gone in the way things are done.
- W | learn the group's ways and keep to them, even the ones that seem odd | v: conformity, tradition | chance: 80
- U | read back through the group's old messages and minutes without asking anyone | v: self-direction, security | closed: approval: reading what was not meant for newcomers; backfire: someone notices, and the nickname 'snoop' sticks | chance: 80
- B | attach yourself to whichever of the two leaders looks likely to win | v: power, security | chance: 70
- R | ignore the pecking order and say what you think from the first week | identity | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 65
- G | sit back for a season and let the group get used to you | v: tradition (humility), security | act: sit back for a season and let the group get used to them | chance: 75
- W>R | ask the leaders to set the rules out plainly, so newcomers can just get on | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 55
- U>G | find out the group's history and the stories behind its odd habits | door | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 80
- B>W | offer to do the dull jobs nobody wants, in return for a say | v: power, conformity | chance: 80
- R>U | try every part of what the group does in your first month | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- G>B | stick close to the friend who brought you in, and grow your corner together | v: security, power | mark: made a friend | act: stick close to the friend who brought them in, and grow their corner together | chance: 75
works: By the end of the season {N} knows where to stand, and someone saves {N} a place.
works: One of the old members starts telling {N} things the newer ones never hear.
fails: {N} steps on the wrong toes early, and it takes months to live down.
fails: {N} still feels like the newest face in the room long after that should have worn off.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- new: the first time it feels like yours (calls RG, s4)

== the first time the group counts on you
tier: everyday
rate: 2
once: yes
stages: juvenile+
age: 12-85
calls: RG
holds: kind:community
tenure: 0-1
tone: joy
life: community, friends
horizon: moment
roles: friend, rival, elder
earth: you have been {title} for less than a year when someone is missing on the big day, and the group turns to you
times: Most new members of a group, once per group, within the first year; groups lean on whoever is there on the day (estimate)
scene: {N} has been {title} for less than a year, and today, on the big day, someone is missing and every face turns to {N}.
scene W: {N} knows the drill, at least on paper, and the group needs someone to do it right. This is what joining meant.
scene U: {N} runs through it in {Ns} head: the order, the timing, the one part that always goes wrong. There are ten minutes left.
scene B: Whoever steps up today will not be the new one any more. {N} can see {rival} thinking the same thing.
scene R: {Ns} heart is pounding and {N} is grinning. This is exactly the moment {N} joined for.
scene G: The group has always found someone when it needed one, the way a flock closes a gap. Today the gap is {Ns} shape.
- W | step in and do it exactly the way it has been practised | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 75
- U | take two minutes to check the plan with the person who usually does it | v: achievement, security | chance: 80
- B | step in, and make sure the leaders know who saved the day | v: power, achievement | chance: 75
- R | throw yourself in and trust the moment to carry you | v: stimulation | act: throw themselves in and trust the moment to carry them | chance: 72
- G | step back and let someone longer in the group take it | v: tradition (humility) | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 88
- W>G | gather everyone first and agree who does what, so nobody is left out | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 75
- U>W | spot the gap in the plan nobody else has seen, and fix it quietly | v: security, achievement | chance: 60
- B>U | let the keenest rival take the hard part, and learn by watching | v: achievement | chance: 90
- R>B | talk over the doubters and take the lead before anyone else can | v: power, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | chance: 65
- G>R | trust your gut over the plan when the moment comes | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 55
works: It goes well, better than well, and for the first time {N} walks home feeling the group is {Ns} own.
works: Someone who has been {title} for years claps {N} on the shoulder and says nothing, which says everything.
fails: It goes wrong in front of everyone, and {N} goes home wishing the floor had opened.
fails: Someone else saves the day in the end, and {N} is left standing to one side.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- settled: taking on more (calls WU, s1)

== the job in the group nobody else wants
tier: everyday
rate: 1
once: yes
stages: juvenile+
age: 12-88
calls: WU
holds: kind:community
tenure: 2-7
tone: mixed
life: community
horizon: years
roles: elder, friend, rival
earth: you have been {title} for a few years, the group needs someone to take on more, and everyone else is looking at their shoes
times: Many members, once per group, two to seven years in, when an organiser steps down; in most groups a small core does most of the work (estimate)
scene: {N} has been {title} for three years, and at the end of the meeting the old organiser says they cannot carry on. Everyone else looks at their shoes.
scene W: Someone has to keep the list, book the hall, mind the money and answer the messages. {N} has always believed that someone should be whoever is asked.
scene U: {N} has seen how the records are kept and the rota is run, and already knows three ways to do it better.
scene B: Whoever does the organising decides who plays, who goes and who waits. That is a kind of power, small as it is.
scene R: {N} joined for the fun of it. Turning it into lists and messages feels like a small death.
scene G: {elder} kept this group alive for twenty years and is tired now. Somebody has to take the weight, or it withers.
- W | say yes, and take on the organising from next week | v: benevolence, conformity | grants: organising people | chance: 26
- U | offer to sort out the records and the money, and nothing else | v: achievement, security | grants: bookkeeping | chance: 80
- B | take the organising on, and fill the main jobs with your own friends | v: power | chance: 75
- R | say no plainly: you came to take part, not to run things | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 82
- G | share the work out among the old hands so no one carries it alone | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 65
- W>U | take the first-aid course the group has needed for years | door | v: benevolence, achievement | grants: first-aid certificate | chance: 85
- U>B | plan a new way of raising money that leaves you in charge of it | v: power, achievement | chance: 60
- B>R | agree to run things only if you get the best spot as well | v: power, hedonism | chance: 60
- R>G | throw a party to bring back the members who drifted away | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 72
- G>W | ask the oldest member to stay on as an adviser while you learn | v: tradition, security | grants: mentor | chance: 80
works: The group comes through the change, and by the end of the year {N} is the one people message with questions.
works: The work turns out to suit {N}, and the group is in better shape than it has been for years.
fails: The work piles up faster than {N} can clear it, and a few members drift away.
fails: An argument over who should have done it splits the group for a season.

